  gaps: [
    "No source read reports a trial comparing the labels' hold rules with one another or with the ESC approach, and no label states the evidence for its thresholds. The prospective studies read on continuing treatment with an asymptomatic LVEF of 40 to 49 per cent are two single-arm studies of 31 and 20 patients, one of them supported by the manufacturer; the other studies ESC and ESMO cite for this advice were not read.",
    "Several labels say their drug has not been studied in people who start with an LVEF below 50 per cent (the combined pertuzumab and trastuzumab injection: below 55 per cent in early breast cancer); how the hold rules apply to them is not settled by any source read.",
    "The labels word their thresholds in different units ('% absolute decrease', '%-points', '10% from baseline'), and the trastuzumab deruxtecan label's 'within 10% from baseline' is given here in its own words because it does not say absolute or relative.",
    "No trastuzumab biosimilar label was read; whether each carries the same cardiac text as Herceptin was not checked. The US label for subcutaneous trastuzumab with hyaluronidase (Herceptin Hylecta) was read and carries the same rule as intravenous Herceptin.",
    "No EU product information for margetuximab was found: the EMA addresses for it returned 404 on 18 November 2026. Its rule on this page is the US label's only.",
    "Tucatinib and neratinib are not covered. Their US labels were read and hold no rule on ejection fraction; the EU neratinib SmPC asks only for cardiac monitoring, including LVEF, as clinically indicated in people with known cardiac risk factors.",
    "The EMA product-information files print no date of revision; the dates given are those of the PDF files. The US labels for trastuzumab, pertuzumab, Phesgo, trastuzumab emtansine and margetuximab print no revision line in the text read, so the SPL effective date is given.",
    "The 2022 ESC guideline was read from the published PDF held in an open university repository, re-used from an earlier round; the publisher's page refuses a script. Its Figure 26 (the management flowchart) was read only through its legend. Its two 2023 errata (10.1093/eurheartj/ehad196 and 10.1093/ehjci/jead080) could not be read: on 18 November 2026 the publisher's site refused a script and neither is in PubMed Central, so whether they touch the recommendations quoted here is not known. The PDF read was generated on 20 March 2023, three days before the first erratum was published. ESC's recommendations name HER2-targeted therapy as a class and do not separate antibodies, conjugates and kinase inhibitors.",
    "The ASCO 2017 guideline was read only as an abstract, which says nothing on holding or restarting HER2-targeted therapy; its full recommendations are not on this page. A 2025 Heart Failure Association consensus statement on preventing cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (10.1002/ejhf.3753) exists and could not be read: on 18 November 2026 the publisher's site refused a script and no open copy was found in PubMed Central.",
    "Which heart failure medicines to use, their doses, and the role of strain imaging and blood biomarkers in deciding to hold are covered only as far as the quoted recommendations go.",
    "The US labels for trastuzumab, pertuzumab, trastuzumab emtansine and margetuximab have no patient guide; their symptom advice is addressed to the prescriber. The patient-facing wording comes from the EU package leaflets and the US Medication Guides for trastuzumab deruxtecan and zanidatamab. The lapatinib texts ask patients to tell their doctor about heart symptoms but give no urgency for them.",
  ],
